Because you have heavy files (like .safetensors, .arrow, .parquet, and .joblib), running git add -A right now without an updated .gitignore would blow up your Git history size to tens of gigabytes and freeze Git.

Here is the bulletproof, 4-step way to safely preserve everything and start a clean refactored branch.
Step 1: Make a Physical Backup on the Server (100% Fail-Safe)

First, make a direct disk-level copy of your entire folder on calc12. This guarantees you cannot lose a single file or intermediate result no matter what happens in Git:
code Bash

# Run this from your home directory (~/)
cd ~
cp -r detection detection_backup_full
cd detection

Step 2: Put the .gitignore in Place First

Create/overwrite your .gitignore right now before making any branch commits. This tells Git to save all your old code and scripts, but ignore the heavy binaries:
code Bash

cat << 'EOF' > .gitignore
# Heavy data, features and caches
data_static/
data/
*.parquet
*.csv
*.arrow
*.joblib
*.db
*.sqlite3

# Model weights & Checkpoints
outputs*/
output/
optuna_trials/
checkpoints/
models/
*.safetensors
*.bin
*.pt
*.pth

# Python & Environment
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
wandb/

# Local workspace
legacy/
scratch/
EOF